H1: rotation ≥ neutral at W4 (both methods), H2: benefit grows W4→W2, H3: visible for both methods but plausibly larger with GPTQ; headline: rotation shifts the collapse point at least one bit-width step lower for at least one method (most plausibly RTN, which has no Hessian compensation to fall back on). These are stated expectations for the analysis to confirm or refute — no outcome is claimed here.

Summary of how the design answers the question: the factorial isolates the rotation as the only differing factor between paired arms (C2 parity, C1 losslessness); the bits axis locates where RTN/GPTQ fail (pre-registered §5); the rotation×bits interaction and collapse-point shift quantify "stabilize and improve where traditional methods fail"; per-layer diagnostics supply the mechanism.", "evidence": "Design is grounded in: (1) Repository observation — /workspace contains only task.json; track.md and all code/data are absent (verified via ls -laR and find), confirming a design-only task grounded in published QuaRot (NeurIPS 2024) methodology and standard RTN/GPTQ practice, with every setup detail named explicitly. (2) All eight researcher resolutions incorporated as fixed decisions: Llama-2-7B primary + Llama-3-8B/Llama-2-13B generality check (§2, §7 cut order); full 2×2 RTN/GPTQ × ±rotation factorial (§3); compute envelope stated as explicit assumptions with a cut-first priority list, never cutting parity/losslessness/W2/pre-registration (§7); 128×2048 C4-train calibration for GPTQ arms only, rotation data-free (§3, control C2 context); non-negotiable quantizer parity as control C2 (§6.1); pre-registered failure = PPL >10× FP16 baseline or non-finite output, with collapse-point reporting (§5); fused-weight offline-folded rotation evaluated through an identical standard forward pass with losslessness gate C1 (§3, §6.1); 3 GPTQ seeds, seq len 2048, WikiText-2+C4 PPL plus zero-shot subset, FP16 re-baselined (§4). (3) Analysis plan covers main effect (H1), rotation×bits (H2), rotation×method (H3), three-way interaction, paired per-seed comparisons with effect sizes, per-layer MSE/dynamic-range mechanism diagnostics, and a W2 case study (§6). Fixed requirements respected throughout: weight-only (no activation quantization), W∈{4,3,2}, RTN and GPTQ baselines. No experiment executed; hypotheses in §8 are expectations, not results.", "assumptions": [], "uncertainties": ["Residual: exact clip-search grid and GPTQ dampening/ordering defaults are stated as proposed values; if the researcher's source specifies different canonical settings, they supersede §3's defaults.", "Residual: availability/licensing of Llama checkpoints under the network-disabled execution environment is assumed but unverified (§7).", "Residual: zero-shot task list may be trimmed per the §7 cut order; the exact surviving subset is budget-dependent.", "Residual: Llama-2-13B vs Llama-3-8B as the surviving second model is decided by the cut order, not fixed in advance."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-c4a0743b46aa49c09ffcd80872c9886e", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-c4a0743b46aa49c09ffcd80872c9886e", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"answer": "FINAL EXPERIMENT DESIGN \u2014 Hadamard Rotation for Weight-Only Quantization (QuaRot-style) at 2\u20134 Bits\n(Design-only deliverable. No experiment has been run and no results are claimed. Grounded in the published QuaRot methodology (NeurIPS 2024) and standard RTN/GPTQ practice, since the sandbox contains no repository code or data.)\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n1. RATIONALE AND RESEARCH QUESTION\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nResearch question: How does QuaRot's Hadamard transformation improve weight-only quantized models at 2\u20134 bit weight precision, and can it stabilize/improve performance where RTN and GPTQ fail?\n\nMechanistic premise (from QuaRot): Large-magnitude activation/weight outliers in transformer linear layers inflate quantization error under uniform integer quantization. QuaRot applies randomized Hadamard rotations (online R1 fused at RMSNorm/attention/MLP boundaries, offline R2/R3/R4 folded into weights) that rotate tensors toward incoherent Gaussian-like distributions, flattening the dynamic range and making both weights and weights-times-activations quantization-friendly. Because a Hadamard transform is orthogonal (H\u00b7H\u1d40 = I, up to scaling), the rotation is lossless in exact arithmetic: the rotated network computes the same function in FP, so any end-to-end quality change is attributable to quantization error reduction, not to the transform itself. This losslessness is the causal lever the experiment isolates.\n\nOperationalization: a 2\u00d72\u00d73 full factorial \u2014 {Hadamard ON/OFF} \u00d7 {RTN, GPTQ} \u00d7 {W4, W3, W2} \u2014 weight-only (activations in native FP16). The design answers both sub-questions: (a) the main effect of rotation on quality across precision and method, and (b) whether rotation stabilizes the 3/2-bit regime where RTN and GPTQ collapse (pre-registered failure/collapse metric, \u00a75).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n2. MODELS AND LAYERS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n- Primary model: Llama-2-7B (decided, \u00a71 of resolutions). FP16 checkpoint re-baselined under the identical eval harness (per resolution 8).\n- Generality check: Llama-3-8B; Llama-2-13B is the fallback second point if the budget-constrained cut order (\u00a77) triggers. Same factorial, reduced replications (see \u00a77).\n- Layer scope for per-layer diagnostics (\u00a76.3): all nn.Linear modules \u2014 q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj (attention) and gate_proj, up_proj, down_proj (MLP). QuaRot-specific placement checks: (i) the second rotation R2 fused into o_proj and down_proj weights (head/input-rotation cancellation), and (ii) verification that fused-weight outputs match the unfused rotated formulation on a probe batch in FP16 (losslessness sanity check, \u00a76.1, control C1).\n- Architecture-transfer (optional, cut-first): Mistral-7B or Qwen2-7B at W3 only, RTN and GPTQ, \u00b1 rotation \u2014 tests whether findings are Llama-specific.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n3. FACTOR GRID AND ARMS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nPer model, 12 core arms (2 rotation \u00d7 2 method \u00d7 3 bit widths), plus baselines and ablations:\n\nCore grid (Llama-2-7B; replicates n=3 seeds for GPTQ calibration draws \u2014 resolution 8; RTN is deterministic, so n=1 with the seed recorded):\n  A1\u2013A3: RTN, no rotation, W4/W3/W2\n  A4\u2013A6: RTN + Hadamard, W4/W3/W2\n  A7\u2013A9: GPTQ, no rotation, W4/W3/W2 (3 seeds each)\n  A10\u2013A12: GPTQ + Hadamard, W4/W3/W2 (3 seeds each)\n\nFixed quantizer configuration across ALL arms (resolution 5 \u2014 non-negotiable parity control):\n  - Weight-only: weights quantized; activations never quantized (QuaRot's 4-bit activation kernels and kernel-level engineering explicitly out of scope \u2014 resolution 7).\n  - Per-channel asymmetric (min-max, int asymmetric) quantization; group size 128; no act-order/Descoteaux; identical clip-search policy (grid search over clip ratio, same grid and selection criterion, e.g., output-MSE on the calibration set, in every arm).\n  - GPTQ: standard Hessian-based error compensation, block size 128, dampening 0.01, same ordering (natural module order), 128 calibration sequences \u00d7 2048 tokens from the C4 training split (resolution 4).\n\nRotation arm (ON): QuaRot-style fused-weight (offline-folded) formulation \u2014 resolution 7:\n  - R1: online Hadamard rotation fused at RMSNorm outputs and attention head boundaries (v- and o-sides); R2 fused into o_proj and down_proj weights; value/head rotations folded per the QuaRot construction.\n  - The rotation is data-free (standard Hadamard matrix with deterministic sign flips), so no calibration data touches the rotation factor \u2014 the same C4 calibration set is used only by GPTQ arms, keeping rotation strictly data-independent (resolution 4).\n  - Both \u00b1rotation arms are evaluated through an identical standard HF-style FP16 forward pass (weights pre-folded on the ON side); no custom kernels. Documented as a faithful weight-only instantiation of QuaRot's rotation; activation-side kernel engineering out of scope.\n  - Losslessness check (control C1): in FP16 (before quantization), fused-rotated model vs vanilla model output KL divergence \u2264 1e-3 and argmax agreement \u2265 99.9% on 32 probe sequences; a violation invalidates the arm's construction before any quantized run.\n\nSecondary ablation (Llama-2-7B only; runs after the core grid):\n  B1: group size \u2014 g=128 (default) vs per-channel (g=none), RTN and GPTQ, \u00b1 rotation, at W4 and W3, and at W2 with GPTQ (W2 per-channel expected to fail for all arms \u2014 that failure is itself informative and expected; it is reported, not excluded).\n  B2: bit-width sweep refinement at W2\u2013W3 boundary (2.25/2.5/2.75 effective bits via group-level mixed precision) only if budget allows \u2014 optional, cut-first after the transfer model.\n\nBaselines: FP16 model re-run under the same harness for WikiText-2, C4, and the zero-shot suite (resolution 8) \u2014 the reference for all relative metrics and the failure threshold.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n4. EVALUATION PROTOCOL\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nPerplexity (primary end-to-end metric): WikiText-2 test and C4 validation, sequence length 2048, identical tokenization/sliding-window settings in the same harness for every arm including FP16 (resolution 8).\nZero-shot accuracy (secondary end-to-end): lm-eval-harness subset \u2014 LAMBADA (openai), ARC-easy, ARC-challenge, PIQA, HellaSwag, WinoGrande; MMLU 5-shot as a stretch item (cut first if budget shrinks). Fixed few-shot/normalization settings per task.\nPer-layer proxies (diagnostic, \u00a76.3): per-linear-module input/output relative MSE vs the FP module on 32 calibration-style sequences; end-to-end hidden-state KL divergence (output logits vs FP16) per arm.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n5. PRE-REGISTERED FAILURE / COLLAPSE METRIC\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nResolution 6, fixed before any run:\n- An arm FAILS at a bit width iff (i) its perplexity exceeds 10\u00d7 the same-model FP16 baseline perplexity under the same harness, or (ii) evaluation produces numerical divergence (non-finite logits/perplexity).\n- Collapse point of a (model, method, \u00b1rotation) condition = the lowest bit width in {4,3,2} at which the condition does NOT fail. Reported as the headline \"stabilization\" outcome: the claim \"rotation stabilizes the regime where RTN/GPTQ fail\" is supported iff the collapse point shifts to a lower width for \u22651 method when rotation is ON, and/or the rotation\u00d7bit-width interaction (\u00a76.2) is significant with the largest effect at W2.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n6. ANALYSIS PLAN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n6.1 Validity controls (gates before analysis):\n  C1 \u2014 rotation losslessness in FP16 (\u00a73): must pass or all rotated arms are invalid.\n  C2 \u2014 quantizer parity audit: single shared quantizer code path; automated assert that every arm's quantizer config (group size, symmetric flag, clip grid, GPTQ hyperparameters) is byte-identical modulo the rotation flag.\n  C3 \u2014 FP16 baseline sanity: baseline perplexities within published tolerance for the checkpoints used.\n  C4 \u2014 GPTQ seed variance gate: if seed-to-seed perplexity SD at W4 exceeds 0.05 perplexity points, investigate before interpreting interactions.\n\n6.2 Primary statistical analysis (per model):\n  - Model: perplexity ~ rotation + method + bits + rotation\u00d7method + rotation\u00d7bits + method\u00d7bits + rotation\u00d7method\u00d7bits, fit on per-seed measurements (GPTQ seeds as replicates; RTN contributes its single deterministic run with seed noted). Analysis on log-perplexity and on relative degradation \u0394PPL = (PPL_arm \u2212 PPL_FP16)/PPL_FP16.\n  - Main effect of rotation (H1): rotation lowers PPL averaged over methods and bits. Interpretation anchored to the interaction (H2), since the question predicts the effect grows as bits shrink.\n  - Rotation\u00d7bits interaction (H2): the rotation benefit must increase monotonically from W4\u2192W2. Contrast tests: rotation effect at W2 vs at W4.\n  - Rotation\u00d7method interaction (H3): whether rotation benefits GPTQ more than RTN (plausible: GPTQ's compensation operates on a better-conditioned Hessian/error landscape after rotation) or vice versa.\n  - Paired comparisons: within each (model, method, bits), paired comparison of \u00b1rotation using the same calibration draws per seed (GPTQ) \u2014 paired t/Wilcoxon on 3 seed pairs with exact permutation p-values given n=3; reported with effect sizes (\u0394PPL, %) rather than null-hypothesis verdicts alone, given the small n.\n  - Zero-shot: same factorial on accuracy; analyzed descriptively per task plus a mean-over-tasks composite, since accuracy at W3/W2 may be floor-degenerate even when PPL differentiates.\n  - Collapse-point analysis (\u00a75) reported per (model, method, \u00b1rotation) as the summary table.\n\n6.3 Per-layer diagnostics (mechanistic evidence):\n  - Layer-wise output MSE (\u00b1rotation) correlated with end-to-end \u0394PPL to show which modules' error reduction drives the global effect; hypothesis: o_proj/down_proj (where the second rotation R2 applies) and layers with the largest pre-rotation weight dynamic range show the largest MSE reduction.\n  - Weight-distribution diagnostics: per-tensor max/mean ratio and kurtosis before/after rotation \u2014 verifies the outlier-flattening mechanism actually engaged.\n  - W2 case study: trace the first module whose output MSE explodes in the no-rotation arms and whether rotation defers it.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n7. FEASIBILITY SECTION (assumptions, not resolved facts)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStated assumptions (explicit, per resolution 3):\n- Hardware: single A100-class GPU (80 GB); CPU-only fallback would invalidate execution of the design \u2014 execution requires this assumed envelope.\n- Budget: order of a few GPU-days for the 7B-scale core grid. Rough envelope: each quantization run (GPTQ, 7B) ~1\u20132 h; each PPL eval ~0.5 h; zero-shot suite ~2\u20134 h per arm. Core grid \u2248 9 (GPTQ arms \u00d73 seeds) + 3 RTN + 3 RTN-rot + 9 GPTQ-rot arms + FP16 baselines \u2248 well within a few GPU-days; the second model and zero-shot breadth are the dominant costs.\n- Cut order if budget shrinks: (1) architecture-transfer model (\u00a72) \u2014 cut first; (2) MMLU and then zero-shot breadth down to LAMBADA+ARC-e+PIQA; (3) ablation B2; (4) second generality model (Llama-2-13B preferred over Llama-3-8B if only one survives, same family as primary); (5) reduce GPTQ seeds 3\u21922. NEVER cut: quantizer parity (C2), losslessness gate (C1), the W2 arms, or the failure-metric pre-registration \u2014 these are load-bearing for the question.\n- Availability: Llama-2-7B/13B and Llama-3-8B checkpoints and WikiText-2/C4/lm-eval datasets are assumed publicly obtainable; if licensing blocks any checkpoint, substitute the nearest open-weight equivalent and record it.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n8. EXPECTED READING (hypotheses, not results)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nH1: rotation \u2265 neutral at W4 (both methods), H2: benefit grows W4\u2192W2, H3: visible for both methods but plausibly larger with GPTQ; headline: rotation shifts the collapse point at least one bit-width step lower for at least one method (most plausibly RTN, which has no Hessian compensation to fall back on). These are stated expectations for the analysis to confirm or refute \u2014 no outcome is claimed here.\n\nSummary of how the design answers the question: the factorial isolates the rotation as the only differing factor between paired arms (C2 parity, C1 losslessness); the bits axis locates where RTN/GPTQ fail (pre-registered \u00a75); the rotation\u00d7bits interaction and collapse-point shift quantify \"stabilize and improve where traditional methods fail\"; per-layer diagnostics supply the mechanism.", "evidence": "Design is grounded in: (1) Repository observation \u2014 /workspace contains only task.json; track.md and all code/data are absent (verified via ls -laR and find), confirming a design-only task grounded in published QuaRot (NeurIPS 2024) methodology and standard RTN/GPTQ practice, with every setup detail named explicitly. (2) All eight researcher resolutions incorporated as fixed decisions: Llama-2-7